--TEST--
An `#[Option]` on a method that declares no command is refused rather than ignored — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6 gives the marker its meaning on a `#[Command]` method's parameter, where
// it is the spelling an argument arrives by. Away from a command there is no
// command line to arrive from, and the refusal is the same one `rule:routing/a-query-parameter-is-declared-like-a-capture`'s
// `#[Query]` gets one file over: both names are on the compiler's closed
// roster, so neither is ignored where nothing reads it.
class Deploy {
    public static function deploy(#[Core\Option] string $target): void {}
}

// The sibling is what the marker is stray of, and one declaration answers the
// question — the command *table* is not consulted and does not have to exist.
class Release {
    #[Core\Command(name: "release", about: "ship it")]
    public static function release(#[Core\Option] string $target): void {}
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0766]: `#[Option] $target` is on a method that declares no command
  --> case.nvs:8:37
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
